    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.4,
    "explanation": "Rider and driver: this dispute goes to a human reviewer, with the SGD 37.55 held rather than collected or refunded. The trip is 1.60 km longer than the recommended route - 22.9 km actually driven against 21.3 km recommended (EV-TRIP-05011) - which is over POL-RD-22's 1.0 km tolerance, so the POL-RD-24 refund matrix is engaged; both sides agree on that, and the GPS trace and event log confirm the vehicle left the PIE at the Eng Neo Avenue exit at 08:12:19 and rejoined at the Bukit Timah Road / CTE slip road at 08:22:49. I also find the reroute traffic-justified under POL-RD-23. The LTA advisory (EV-ADVIS-05011-01) is an ACCIDENT on the Pan-Island Expressway towards Changi after the Adam Road exit, valid 07:44:37 to 08:54:37, which overlaps the trip window and lies on the PIE stretch of the recommended route in the direction the trip was travelling; the driver's in-trip message describes the same incident. That removes rows (c), (d) and (e). The case therefore stands on row (a) or row (b), and both turn on one question: did the driver inform the rider before or at the point of deviation, as POL-RD-21 requires? The record cannot settle that at the confidence this policy requires. The only artifact that speaks to it is the chat log EV-CHAT-05011, and the platform graded it impaired: weight_multiplier 0.4, issue code CROSS_SOURCE_CONFLICT. MSG-05011-003 carries a driver-device sent time of 08:10:49 - before the deviation - and a server delivery time of 08:14:19 - after it - so the same entry supports both readings, and the rider's reply 'ok can' (MSG-05011-004) is at 08:14:39 on either reading. The driver's account that he messaged before the exit, and the trip record's in-trip message list, do not cure this: POL-RD-21 reads the chat log, the statement is an account standing in for the conflicted timing rather than independent evidence, and the trip record's list repeats the same chat entry rather than measuring it separately, so it is not a second independent source under POL-GEN-02(2). Because the fact that decides between rows (a) and (b) is carried by an artifact at 0.4, POL-GEN-02(2) caps confidence at 0.4, below the 0.80 that POL-GEN-22 and POL-RD-25 require for a 37.55 SGD dispute. On the money: row (a) would leave the 37.55 SGD metered fare (EV-FARE-05011) standing, and row (b) would return only the excess distance fare - 1.60 km x 0.72 SGD per km = 1.15 SGD - and no more; no row returns the surge of 7.65 SGD, the time fare of 6.21 SGD, or any other part of the 37.55 SGD, so the full fare the rider asks for cannot be awarded. Neither row can be ruled at the required confidence, so under POL-GEN-26 the case is escalated for the open question - whether the notice preceded the turn - to be decided by a human reviewer. No safety flag and no fraud flag applies, so the escalation rests on POL-GEN-26 and POL-RD-25 alone. No referral is attached: the route-deviation policy contains no referral clause."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}

